Introduction {#introduction}
===========================

Different application in the RDF domain make use of different vocabularies, 
annotation models (reification, triple terms, named graphs, singleton properties, resource referencing) 
and annotation methods (concrete applications of models with added behavior, such as VCs, nanopublications, ...), 
forming a heterogeneous ecosystem of annotations when integrating contextualized data from different applications into local RDF knowledge graphs.

The aim of Context Associations, is to present a minimal annotaiton method for RDF, 
that can be reused by other annotation methods for defining concrete associations of contextual information 
to well-defined target boundaries in the form of named graphs within the bounds of the local RDF knowledge graph.

The specification defines
<ol>
  <li>A core data model, that defines the rules in generating named graphs, and the terms used to link them.</li>
  <li>An encoding guide, that defines how annotation methods in RDF can be converted to or made compatible with the Context Association format.</li>
  <li>A decoding guide, specifying the SPARQL setup necessary to retrieve all data graphs and associated metadata graphs for which a user query holds.</li>
</ol>

This way, Context Associations defines a **minimal pattern for exchange, storage and query** of statements and their associated meta-information in local RDF graphs. The integration of remote resources through URI references are disregarded, as no uniform semantics or approach exists as to how these should be managed.

Terminology {#terminology}
=========================

The following terminology is used throughout the specification document:

- **context association**: The association of a source named graph, targeting a target named graph, though a contained anchor triple.

- **dataset**: An RDF dataset as defined in RDF Concepts. [W3C](https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf12-concepts/)

- **named graph**: An RDF named graph as defined in RDF Concepts, defined by a (name - RDF graph) pair. [W3C](https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf12-concepts/)

- **blank node graph**: An RDF named graph, using a graph name that is blank node identifier. [W3C](https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf12-concepts/)

- **graph name**: The IRI or blank node that names a named graph in the dataset. [W3C](https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf12-concepts/)

- **target graph**: A named graph in the dataset that is the subject of contextual statements.

- **context graph**: A named graph that contains an anchor triple statement identifying a target graph.

- **anchor triple**: A triple statement of the form `sourceGraph ca:aboutGraph targetGraph`, defining the context association between the two named graphs.

- **graph reference**: An RDF term that is used as a graph name in the dataset.

Structural Vocabulary and Predicate Stratification (normative) {#structuralvocabulary}
======================================================================================

Context Associations defines a **closed set of structural predicates**. These predicates are used exclusively to describe relationships between graphs, encodings, and context units.

> Structural predicates **MUST NOT contribute to the data content of a graph**. They exist solely to describe structure, provenance, or grouping, and MUST be ignored when interpreting the data semantics of RDF graphs.

## anchor properties

- `ca:aboutGraph` — anchor predicate defining context associations between named graphs.

## preservation properties
- `ca:originalName` — indicates the original name identifier of the for decoding purposes.
- `ca:DefaultGraph` — defines the original structure as the default graph of the original RDF dataset.
<!--
// - `ca:encodedFrom` - defines the original RDF structure from which the contents were encoded to a named graph.
// - `ca:NamedGraph` — defines the original graph identifier as the default graph of the original RDF dataset.
// - `ca:TripleTerm` — defines the original graph identifier as the default graph of the original RDF dataset.
// - `ca:Reification` — defines the original graph identifier as the default graph of the original RDF dataset.
// - `ca:SingletonProperty` — defines the original graph identifier as the default graph of the original RDF dataset.
-->

Minimal semantics (normative) {#minimalsemantics}
=================================================

Let **SP** be the set of all structural predicates defined above.

For any named graph `G`, the **content triples** of `G`, denoted `CT(G)`, are defined as:

```
CT(G) = { ⟨s,p,o⟩ ∈ G | p ∉ SP }
```

Given an RDF dataset `D`:

- If a named graph `(C, c)` contains an anchor triple `(c, ca:aboutGraph, t)`, then `C` defines a context association whose target graph is `(t, T)`.
- The **target graph** is interpreted as `CT(T)`.
- All triples in `CT(C)` are interpreted as **context statements** over `CT(T)`.
- Context associations are **not transitive**. Any contextual relation should directly be indicated in the context graph.

A context graph MAY itself be referenced as a target graph. In that case, its interpretation is still based on its content triples `CT(C)`.





Examples (informative) {#examples}
==================================

## Simple metadata about a data graph (N-Quads)

```turtle
_:dataG {
  <https://example.org/x> <https://example.org/y> <https://example.org/z> .
}
_:ctxG {
  _:ctxG <https://w3id.org/context-associations#aboutGraph> _:dataG .
  _:dataG <https://example.org/pred/retrievedFrom> <https://source.example/> .
}
```

## Chaining (context about context) and avoiding aliasing

```turtle
_:dataG {
  <https://example.org/x> <https://example.org/y> <https://example.org/z>  .
}

_:policyG {
  _:policyG <https://w3id.org/context-associations#aboutGraph> _:dataG  .
  <https://example.org/policy/pol1> <https://example.org/ns#type> <https://example.org/ns#Policy> .
  <https://example.org/policy/pol1> <https://example.org/ns#allowedPurpose> <https://example.org/ns#Analytics> .
}

_:sigG {
  _:sigG <https://w3id.org/context-associations#aboutGraph> _:policyG .
  <https://example.org/sig/sig1> <https://example.org/ns#type> <https://example.org/ns#Signature> .
  <https://example.org/sig/sig1> <https://example.org/ns#signatureValue> "..." .
  <https://example.org/sig/sig1> <https://example.org/ns#verificationKey> <did:example:123#k1> .
}
```

Graph identifiers and exchange profiles {#identifiersandprofiles}
=================================================================

## Blank node graph names (recommended default)

Graph names may be blank nodes. [W3C](https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf12-concepts/)

This specification recommends using blank nodes for graph names participating in context associations when the goal is to avoid unintended  
co-reference/merge across unrelated datasets.

## Skolemized identifiers (optional)

Implementations may replace blank nodes with Skolem IRIs, and systems doing so should mint globally unique IRIs for each blank node.  
[W3C](https://www.w3.org/2011/rdf-wg/wiki/Skolemisation)

Skolemization can improve addressability and compatibility with systems/protocols that require graph IRIs (for example, managing graphs via  
the SPARQL Graph Store Protocol assumes IRI-addressable graphs over HTTP). [W3C](https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql12-graph-store-protocol)

**Import guidance (non-normative):**

-   When importing datasets across untrusted boundaries, a receiver may relabel incoming skolem graph identifiers to fresh local identifiers  
    to prevent accidental or adversarial co-reference, while preserving the sender identifier via `ca:sourceGraphName`.
    

Processing algorithm (normative)
================================

A conforming **CA processor** must implement the following:

1.  Parse the input as an RDF dataset.
    
2.  For each named graph `C` with graph name `c`, find triples `(c, ca:aboutGraph, t)` **within `C`**.
    
3.  If there is exactly one such triple, treat `C` as a context graph targeting `t`. Otherwise, `C` is not a valid context graph under this specification.
    
4.  The association output is a set of pairs `(c -> t)` plus the set of context triples in `C` excluding the anchor triple.
    
5.  A processor must not assume that `t` is globally meaningful outside the dataset scope unless an application profile explicitly says so  
    (e.g., trusted skolem IDs).
    

Note (informative): Applications that traverse context-association chains should avoid infinite recursion by tracking already-visited graph references.

